<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- One page, one URL, and deliberately nothing else.

     <changefreq> and <priority> used to sit below the <loc>. Google ignores
     both outright, and has said so publicly for years, so they were tuning
     precisely nothing while reading as though this file had been tuned. That is
     the worse failure of the two: config that does nothing but looks deliberate
     invites the next person to adjust it instead of fixing what actually ranks.

     <lastmod> IS read — but only counts while it stays accurate, and a date
     typed in here by hand is wrong from the very next commit onward. Google
     discounts a sitemap whose lastmod it has learned not to trust, which leaves
     you worse off than omitting it. So <loc> alone is both the complete and the
     honest form for a single-page site. If this ever serves more than one URL,
     that is the point to stamp lastmod at deploy time from the commit date —
     automatically, never by hand.

     Note that nothing currently advertises this file to anyone: robots.txt is
     read per ORIGIN, and this is a project site published under /Portfolio/, so
     the Sitemap: line in it has never been fetched. The comment in robots.txt
     has the full explanation. Submit this URL in Search Console directly. -->
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  <url>
    <loc>https://bu1ldr.github.io/Portfolio/</loc>
  </url>
</urlset>
